# How this cloud publishes the node's gateway.
#
# A passthrough Network Load Balancer, which is what a Service of type
# LoadBalancer produces on GKE without being asked, and that is the whole of C23
# arriving in a manifest. An HTTP(S) load balancer terminates TLS: it would
# present its own certificate to a peer that pinned this node's (C12) and
# consume the caller's certificate that ReplicateOperations identifies a peer
# by. This one forwards TCP and reads nothing, which is the only thing port 9443
# can survive.
#
# All three ports go through it, including the two the gateway does terminate.
# There is no reason to split them across two load balancers: what terminates
# 443 is the node's own gateway either way, and a second balancer would be a
# second name in DNS for one node.
#
# The address is the one this stack reserved, so it is written into DNS in the
# same apply and survives the Service being recreated — which matters because a
# node's address is what every peer in the federation recorded.
#
# Generated by deploy/terraform/gcp. Edit the template, not this file.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: quire-gateway
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  loadBalancerIP: ${address}
  # Cluster and not Local, and the cost is stated rather than discovered: the
  # node sees the load balancer's address as the client's, not the caller's.
  # Nothing here depends on that. A peer is identified by the certificate it
  # presents and a device by its bearer token, and neither is an address — which
  # is the same property that lets a node be reached through a NAT.
  externalTrafficPolicy: Cluster
